## The runtime

This game stands on no engine, so the runtime under it is yours to write: the
frame loop and the delta time it measures, the canvas fit, input from both the
keyboard and the pointer, audio cues, and the debug overlay. Where this
specification speaks of the runtime it means that layer, and it states only what
Gantry needs from it. How the layer is structured is yours to decide.

Rendering is also yours. `three` is a dependency of the project for the 3D
scene, and `@test-cabinet/voxel-runtime` for decoding the models the build
produces; how the scene is put together on top of them is your design.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-3d")}}
## The runtime

The game is built on the {{engine.name}} engine, which documents itself in the
`engine/` directory at the root of this repository. It owns the frame loop and
the delta time the game is given, the canvas fit, the input actions the menus
and the camera are driven by, the pointer, audio cues, loading the produced
files and decoding them, and the debug overlay. Read its documentation before
you start. This specification states only what is specific to Gantry.

The engine holds the game's state, by value: each frame it hands `update` the
current state read-only and keeps what `update` returns as the next, so a frame
builds the next state from the current one rather than writing into it.

The picture is the game's, built through the engine's. The engine owns the
renderer, the retained scene it draws, the camera it draws through, and the 2D
screen layer it composites over that picture; the game fills that scene, poses
that camera, and draws the readouts on that layer, all from the state it was
handed. `three` is a dependency of the project and the engine's peer, so the
engine and the game's own code share the one copy. The pointer reaches the game
from the engine in the stage's logical units, with its press and release edges;
`specs/controls.md` states what Gantry does with them.
{{else}}
## The runtime

The game is built on the {{engine.name}} engine, which documents itself in the
`engine/` directory at the root of this repository. It is a gameplay framework
as much as a runtime: the game is written as subclasses of its classes, a game
instance, a game mode and the game state it names, actors and their components,
and the controllers that drive them, and the engine constructs, ticks, and
renders them in a fixed order. It owns the frame loop and the delta time each
tick is given, rendering and the camera, the canvas fit, collision detection,
the input actions the menus and the camera are driven by, the pointer, audio
cues, loading the produced files and decoding them, and the debug overlay. Read
its documentation before you start. This specification states only what is
specific to Gantry.

The framework's state is live. A game state, an actor's transform, a component's
offset, and the world's camera are objects the game writes into, so a tick
advances the fields it moves in place rather than returning a replacement.

The picture is the engine's. What is drawn is described as render components on
actors, which the engine collects and draws each frame: meshes, lights, and the
produced models in world space, a game-built `three` subtree for geometry Gantry
draws itself, and text, shapes, sprites, and direct drawing on the screen layer
over them. The camera is the world's own, which the game poses. `three` is a
dependency of the project and the engine's peer, so the engine and the game's
own code share the one copy. The pointer reaches the game through a player
controller, already in the stage's logical units and carrying its press and
release edges, and the world camera casts a point on the stage into a ray
through the yard; `specs/controls.md` states what Gantry does with them.

The engine detects collisions between the colliders a game declares and reports
the pairs; deciding what a report means is the game's. Gantry decides its own
failures without it: every cause in the vocabulary `specs/statics.md` fixes,
the two obstacle strikes and the ground strike included, is settled by the
tests that file states, at the stage of the tick `specs/program.md` puts them
in, over the structure and the load as that stage found them.
{{/if}}

## The project you build in

The project at the root of this repository is already wired up, and its
`README.md` describes it. It is a TypeScript project built with Vite, tested
with Vitest, linted with ESLint, and formatted with Prettier. That toolchain is
fixed and supplied.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### What you implement

Everything under `src/`, and everything under `assets/`. Neither directory
exists yet.

`index.html` loads `/src/main.ts` as its entry point, so that module is where
your build starts. Beyond that the structure is yours: the frame loop and the
canvas fit, keyboard and pointer input, audio, the diagnostics overlay, the
debug and automation surface, the figures this specification fixes, and the game
itself.

The game's state is yours to shape. `specs/state.md` states what it must carry,
and `specs/instrumentation.md` states the one contract fixed over it.

### What stays as it is

- `index.html`, the page and the canvas the stage is fitted into.
- `.tcab/`, holding the vendored `@test-cabinet/voxel-runtime` that
  `package.json` resolves that dependency from.
- The configuration: `tsconfig.json`, `vite.config.ts`, `vitest.config.ts`,
  `eslint.config.js`, `.prettierrc.json`, `.prettierignore`, and `.gitignore`.

`package.json` takes one kind of change: a dependency you genuinely need,
added along with the updated `package-lock.json`. Its existing entries and
scripts stay as they are.

### Every figure comes from this specification

Every number, identifier, key binding, cue name, and piece of screen copy this
specification fixes is stated here under a name. Anything this specification
does not name is yours to decide.

### Your own tests

Write unit tests for what you build, beside your sources as `src/**/*.test.ts`.
`npm test` runs them in process with Vitest, in Node, with coverage measured
over `src/`. A test drives the game over its own clock for a counted number of
ticks, so it needs no browser and no WebGL, and `@napi-rs/canvas` is installed
for a test that wants a real 2D context to draw through.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-3d")}}
### What you implement

`src/game.ts`, any new modules you add beside it under `src/`, and everything
under `assets/`, which does not exist yet.

`src/game.ts` exports `game`, a `Game<GantryState, GantryDebugApi>`:
`initialize` builds the state and the debug surface once and returns them
together as `[state, debug]`, `update` takes the current state as a read-only
view (`DeepReadonly<GantryState>`) and returns the next state, advanced against
the frame's delta time in seconds, and `render` is handed that next state,
read-only again, and draws it into the engine's scene and over its screen layer.
The engine holds the state by value and replaces it with what each `update`
returns, so a frame builds the next state from the current one rather than
writing into it, and nothing but `update` and the debug surface's poses advances
the game. All three functions throw `"not implemented"` as they stand. Implement
them.

`src/game.ts` also declares and exports `GantryState` and the types it is built
from, exactly as `specs/state.md` states them, and `GantryDebugApi`, the debug
and automation surface `specs/instrumentation.md` fixes.

`src/game.ts` also exports `BACKGROUND`, a CSS color string: the stage
background. `src/main.ts` hands it to the engine as the color the canvas is
cleared to each frame, so the letterbox bars around the stage match the yard's
sky. The seeded stub exports a placeholder; replace it with the color your yard
uses.

### What stays as it is

- `src/main.ts`, the entry point. It stands the engine up over the page's
