### What you implement

`src/game.ts`, any new modules you add beside it under `src/`, and everything
under `assets/`, which does not exist yet.

`src/game.ts` declares and exports `GantryState`, the class the whole of the
game's authoritative state is held in, a subclass of the engine's `GameState`
carrying every field `specs/state.md` fixes, and `GantryDebugApi`, the debug and
automation surface `specs/instrumentation.md` fixes.

`src/game.ts` exports `game`, a `GameDefinition<GantryDebugApi>`: the game
instance class, a level registry holding the single level the game runs in, and
`startLevel` naming it. The engine opens that one level and the game never opens
another; every screen is a value of the state's `screen` field, so the world,
its mode, and its game state live for the whole session. The instance's
`initialize` registers the actions against their bindings, defines the cues,
loads the produced models and sounds, and returns the debug surface, which the
engine holds and hands back from `engine.debug`. The level's game mode runs the
screens and the rules: its `gameStateClass` is `GantryState`, so the engine
builds that state when the world opens; its `beginPlay` adds a single player
possessing nothing, whose controller is where the frame's actions and the
pointer are read, and registers the diagnostic sources
`specs/instrumentation.md` lists. As seeded, the definition is a stub whose
instance's `initialize` throws `"not implemented"`. Implement it.

`GantryState` is the whole of the authoritative game. The actors, components,
and controllers you write draw it and drive it and hold nothing authoritative
of their own, so the yard is described rather than repainted: the ground and
the light, the lattice and envelope aids, the members colored by utilization,
the hoist cable, the pads, the loads and the produced models, and every readout
over them are render components on actors.

`src/game.ts` also exports `BACKGROUND`, a CSS color string: the stage
background. `src/main.ts` hands it to the engine as the color the whole canvas
is cleared to each frame, so the letterbox bars around the stage match the
yard's sky. The seeded stub exports a placeholder; replace it with the color
your yard uses.

